# SABOTAGE: the erasure-completeness CDC mirror scan goes FAIL-OPEN again — the blanket
# `rescue _ -> false` is restored on `Samen.Cdc.LocalPostgres.decryptable?/2`. That private
# helper is the per-token filter `scan_no_plaintext/2` runs over every `vt_*` token the
# mirror holds for a subject; the scanner itself is fail-closed BY DESIGN (its own
# `rescue e -> {:leaks, ["cdc mirror scan failed: …"]}` reports any raise as a leak), and
# the blanket rescue one frame below defeats it: it catches the raise first and answers
# "not decryptable" = SAFE. So an infrastructure failure mid-scan — vault/KMS unreachable,
# repo misconfigured, a bug under `Vault.reveal/2` — is classified per token as "no leak",
# the outer fail-closed rescue never fires, and when every token errors the scan returns
# `{:ok, :no_plaintext}`: a merely-unreachable vault reported as a PROVEN erasure.
#
# Load-bearing because this IS the erasure-completeness verifier read by
# `test/erasure_test.exs`, `test/shred_key_material_test.exs` and
# `driftwood/test/crypto_shred_gameday_test.exs`. A false `:no_plaintext` there is a false
# "erasure verified" — exactly the lie the fail-honest contract (ADR-014/024/026) forbids:
# an error you cannot classify must fail CLOSED.
#
# The distinction the fix rests on: `Vault.reveal/2` answers a legitimately shredded,
# absent or corrupt token with an ERROR TUPLE (`{:error, :shredded | :absent | :not_found |
# :unavailable | :decrypt_failed}`), never a raise — so no correct outcome needs a rescue
# here at all, and the sibling live-tier scanner `Samen.Vault.scan_no_plaintext/2` has
# none either.
#
# Flips RP-E ONLY, which is what makes the red mean what it says: RP-E's two POSITIVE
# CONTROLS stay GREEN under this sabotage — a genuinely shredded token still scans
# `{:ok, :no_plaintext}` (it DENIES with an error tuple, it never raises) and a genuinely
# decryptable token still reports `{:leaks, [token]}`. So RP-E fails because the raise
# path is swallowed, not because the scan, the vault or the mirror broke.
#
# Replaces one contiguous comment-plus-function block with the pre-fix body; the file still
# compiles under `--warnings-as-errors`. Touches local_postgres.ex ALONE.
# APP: samen_core
# TEST_FILES: test/cdc_mirror_test.exs
# MUST_FAIL: RP-E: a reveal that RAISES mid-scan is a LEAK
diff --git a/samen_core/lib/samen/cdc/local_postgres.ex b/samen_core/lib/samen/cdc/local_postgres.ex
--- a/samen_core/lib/samen/cdc/local_postgres.ex
+++ b/samen_core/lib/samen/cdc/local_postgres.ex
@@ -198,31 +198,25 @@ defmodule Samen.Cdc.LocalPostgres do
       rows
       |> List.flatten()
       |> Enum.filter(fn v -> is_binary(v) and String.starts_with?(v, "vt_") end)
     end)
   end
 
-  # Is this mirror token still decryptable? ONLY an `{:ok, _}` reveal means yes.
-  #
-  # NO `rescue` here, deliberately (and matching `Samen.Vault.scan_no_plaintext/2`,
-  # the sibling live-tier scanner, which also lets raises through). `Vault.reveal/2`
-  # answers a legitimately shredded, absent or corrupt token with an ERROR TUPLE —
-  # `{:error, :shredded | :absent | :not_found | :unavailable | :decrypt_failed}` —
-  # never a raise, so every raise reaching here is an infrastructure failure this
-  # function cannot classify (vault/KMS unreachable, repo misconfigured, a bug in
-  # the reveal path). A blanket `rescue _ -> false` would answer "not decryptable"
-  # = SAFE for exactly those cases, shadowing `scan_no_plaintext/2`'s fail-closed
-  # rescue: if every token errored, the scan returned `{:ok, :no_plaintext}` and a
-  # merely-unreachable vault was reported as a PROVEN erasure to the erasure-
-  # completeness callers (`erasure_test.exs`, `shred_key_material_test.exs`,
-  # driftwood's crypto-shred gameday). Letting the raise propagate hands it to that
-  # rescue, which reports it as a leak — fail-honest per ADR-014/024/026: an error
-  # you cannot classify fails CLOSED. Red: cdc_mirror_test.exs RP-E (+ its two
-  # controls: a shredded token still scans clean, a live one still reports a leak).
+  # SABOTAGE (fail-open): the blanket `rescue _ -> false` is BACK on `decryptable?/2`.
+  # Every raise out of the reveal path — vault/KMS unreachable, repo misconfigured, a
+  # bug under `Vault.reveal/2` — is swallowed and answered "not decryptable", i.e.
